    "documentation": {
      "format": "markdown",
      "content": "# Telco Customer Churn\n\nThis dataset presents a governed **binary-classification** problem built around customer-account records from a telecommunications provider. The active Atlas release estimates the probability that `Churn = Yes` from service, contract, billing, payment, tenure, and charge information.\n\nThe experience is intended to make both the dataset and the prediction semantics understandable. It should not be interpreted as evidence that any individual feature causes churn, nor as a validated retention policy.\n\n## Dataset overview\n\nThe companion scientific study acquires the dataset from the Kaggle handle `blastchar/telco-customer-churn`.\n\n| Item                              |                 Value |\n| --------------------------------- | --------------------: |\n| Customer accounts                 |                 7,043 |\n| Source columns                    |                    21 |\n| Runtime model inputs              |                    19 |\n| Identifier excluded from modeling |          `customerID` |\n| Target                            |               `Churn` |\n| Positive class                    |                 `Yes` |\n| Negative class                    |                  `No` |\n| Positive-class prevalence         |                26.54% |\n| Problem type                      | Binary classification |\n\nEach row represents one customer account. The model uses 19 features covering customer profile, relationship tenure, phone and internet services, online services and support, contract terms, billing preferences, payment method, monthly charges, and accumulated charges.\n\nThe identifier `customerID` is excluded from model inputs, and `Churn` is used only as the target.\n\n## Analytical question\n\nThe analysis asks whether the information recorded for a customer account contains predictive signal for distinguishing accounts with `Churn = Yes` from accounts with `Churn = No`.\n\nThe active Atlas release returns the probability assigned to the positive class and applies its governed decision semantics separately to produce a predicted outcome.\n\nThis is a predictive question rather than a causal one. A feature can help separate classes without showing that changing that feature would change a customer's future behavior.\n\n## Data quality and preparation\n\nThe source contains 7,043 unique customer accounts. In the companion study, the principal source-quality issue was limited to 11 blank raw values in `TotalCharges`. Every one of those blanks occurred for an account with zero tenure, and the study deterministically materialized them as `0.0` before downstream modeling. No rows were removed and no generic mean, median, mode, or learned imputation rule was introduced by that study.\n\nThat preparation fact belongs to the scientific study. It is not a second runtime rule for Atlas. The active Atlas release remains governed by its own execution and public contracts.\n\nFor the active release, the model uses a stratified 70% / 15% / 15% train-validation-test policy with random seed `0`:\n\n| Partition  |  Rows | Role                           |\n| ---------- | ----: | ------------------------------ |\n| Train      | 4,930 | Initial model fitting          |\n| Validation | 1,056 | Governed validation evaluation |\n| Final test | 1,057 | One-time final evaluation      |\n| Final fit  | 5,986 | Train plus validation          |\n\nThe final-test partition was not used for fitting, model selection, hyperparameter selection, or threshold selection.\n\nThe active execution contract declares one-hot categorical encoding and standardized numerical handling. The active release uses a fixed HistGradientBoosting configuration rather than performing a new model search inside Atlas.\n\n## Target distribution\n\nThe active Atlas visualization reports 5,174 `No` observations and 1,869 `Yes` observations. The positive class therefore represents about 26.54% of the dataset.\n\nThis imbalance is large enough that a single accuracy-like score would provide an incomplete view of performance. The active release consequently exposes discrimination and positive-class metrics including ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, and F1.\n\n## Key exploratory findings\n\nThe following findings come from the companion scientific study and describe associations in this dataset snapshot.\n\n### Contract term is strongly associated with churn\n\nObserved churn rates differ substantially across contract categories:\n\n| Contract       | Observed churn rate |\n| -------------- | ------------------: |\n| Month-to-month |              42.71% |\n| One year       |              11.27% |\n| Two year       |               2.83% |\n\n![Observed churn rate by contract category](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-telco-customer-churn/main/docs/images/contract_churn_rate_by_category.png)\n\nThis pattern is analytically important, but it does not show that assigning a longer contract would independently reduce churn. Contract choice can reflect other customer characteristics and selection effects.\n\n### Churn is concentrated in shorter observed relationships\n\nAcross tenure quantiles in the companion study, churn decreases from approximately 58.4% in the earliest-tenure quantile to approximately 3.5% in the latest.\n\n![Observed churn rate by tenure quantile](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-telco-customer-churn/main/docs/images/tenure_churn_rate_by_quantile.png)\n\nTenure is measured in months. It is predictive in this snapshot, but it is also mechanically related to how long an account has remained active, so the observed relationship must not be presented as a causal retention effect.\n\n### Service, support, and billing information add predictive signal\n\nThe scientific EDA identifies contract type, online security, technical support, internet service, and payment method among the strongest categorical associations with churn.\n\nThe active Atlas release provides a separate governed model-based view through permutation importance scored with ROC-AUC. In that release, `Contract` and `tenure` are the two most important displayed features, followed by `MonthlyCharges`, `InternetService`, and `TotalCharges`.\n\nPermutation importance measures predictive dependence of this fitted model on the evaluated feature. It is not causal importance and does not imply that intervening on the feature would change churn.\n\n## Model selection evidence\n\nThe companion study compared a prior-only dummy baseline with Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, and HistGradientBoostingClassifier. Average Precision was the study's primary selection metric because `Churn = Yes` is the minority class. HistGradientBoosting and Logistic Regression formed a practical tie, and the study selected HistGradientBoosting using lower validation Brier Score as the predefined tie-break.\n\nThe active Atlas release does **not** rerun that scientific model search. Its execution contract fixes the `hist_gradient_boosting` family and the following configuration:\n\n| Hyperparameter      | Active release value |\n| ------------------- | -------------------: |\n| `learning_rate`     |                 0.03 |\n| `max_iter`          |                  200 |\n| `max_depth`         |                    3 |\n| `max_leaf_nodes`    |                    7 |\n| `min_samples_leaf`  |                   40 |\n| `l2_regularization` |                  1.0 |\n\nAtlas evaluates this fixed configuration using ROC-AUC as the primary metric, with F1 and PR-AUC as secondary metrics.\n\n## Active Atlas release evaluation\n\nThe following values come from the active Atlas release, not from the companion study:\n\n| Metric  | Validation | Final test |\n| ------- | ---------: | ---------: |\n| ROC-AUC |     0.8385 | **0.8545** |\n| PR-AUC  |     0.6517 | **0.6768** |\n| F1      |     0.5702 | **0.6130** |\n\nThe final-test evaluation contains 1,057 rows and was completed once. Its records state that the final test was not used for fitting, model selection, hyperparameter selection, or threshold selection.\n\nThe Home Card and Performance Summary should highlight **ROC-AUC = 0.8545**, because ROC-AUC is the primary metric governed by this release.\n\n## Understanding the highlighted metrics\n\n### ROC-AUC — 0.8545\n\nROC-AUC measures how well the model ranks positive and negative examples across possible decision thresholds. Higher values indicate better overall discrimination. The active release declares ROC-AUC as its primary evaluation metric.\n\n### PR-AUC — 0.6768\n\nPR-AUC summarizes the precision-recall trade-off for the positive class. It is especially informative here because `Churn = Yes` is the minority class. Higher values indicate better positive-class ranking performance.\n\n### F1 — 0.6130\n\nF1 combines precision and recall at the release's governed decision threshold. Unlike ROC-AUC and PR-AUC, it depends on the particular threshold used to convert probabilities into class decisions.\n\nThese metrics describe performance within this evaluation contract. They do not establish future production performance, intervention effectiveness, or economic value.\n\n## Predictive interaction and result interpretation\n\nThe active release exposes a public prediction interaction with 19 model inputs.\n\nIts primary result is the probability assigned to the positive class:\n\n`Churn = Yes`\n\nThe release-governed decision threshold is:\n\n`0.5`\n\nProbabilities are also mapped to the release's risk interpretation bands using cut points at `0.35` and `0.65`, producing low, medium, and high risk labels.\n\nThe risk band and predicted class answer different presentation questions. The band summarizes where the probability falls, while the predicted outcome is determined by the `0.5` decision threshold.\n\nThese thresholds and bands belong to the active Atlas release. They must not be replaced by the companion study's separate educational threshold.\n\nA prediction should be read as a model estimate for an account described by the supplied features. It is not a causal explanation, a guarantee that churn will occur, or a validated instruction to contact, retain, discount, or otherwise intervene on a customer.\n\n## What this dataset demonstrates\n\nThis Dataset Detail demonstrates how Atlas can combine:\n\n* a governed binary-classification release;\n* explicit positive-class and decision semantics;\n* a public input contract;\n* a model-based feature-importance view;\n* final-test and validation metrics;\n* presentation-only Predict View customization;\n* scientific context from an external study without making that study a runtime dependency.\n\nIt also illustrates why scientific exploration and promoted runtime behavior must remain distinct. The study can explain why a model family was investigated, while the active Atlas release remains the authority for the model, metrics, threshold, input contract, and result semantics actually presented by Atlas.\n\n## Limitations\n\n* The evaluation uses a stratified random snapshot rather than a temporal or prospective holdout, so temporal generalization is not established.\n* The dataset is a single observational snapshot; external representativeness and future distribution stability are unconfirmed.\n* Production-time availability, latency, and consistency of the 19 model inputs have not been established.\n* The active `0.5` threshold is a governed model decision threshold, not a demonstrated business-optimal retention policy.\n* False-positive and false-negative business costs, customer value, campaign capacity, and intervention cost are not part of the active evaluation.\n* The active release does not publish a calibration study sufficient to treat predicted probabilities as operationally calibrated risk estimates.\n* No intervention-uplift analysis shows that acting on high predicted churn probability would improve retention.\n* No subgroup fairness or deployment-stability assessment is established.\n* No drift-monitoring or scheduled retraining policy is demonstrated by these artifacts.\n* Associations and permutation importance must not be interpreted as causal effects.\n* The evaluation does not establish production safety, automated decisioning validity, or effectiveness of customer-retention actions.\n\n## Responsible interpretation\n\nThe active Atlas release shows useful discrimination for identifying patterns associated with `Churn = Yes` within this dataset and evaluation contract. Contract term, tenure, charges, internet service, and related service variables provide predictive signal, but neither the model nor the scientific study establishes that changing those variables will cause churn to decrease.\n\nUse the result as a transparent predictive demonstration. Any operational retention policy would require prospective validation, confirmed production feature availability, cost-sensitive decision analysis, calibration assessment, fairness review, monitoring, and evidence that interventions based on the prediction are beneficial.\n"
    }
  },
  "source_draft_schema_version": "1.0.0"
}